+
+# Serve post search and creation to assistants over MCP
+
+## Context
+
+Authors write with their own AI assistants and want them to search the
+blog and draft posts. Assistants reach other services over MCP. We do
+not want a chatbot in the blog, a second implementation of search or
+post creation, or protocol code of our own to maintain.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **The tools are the existing routes.** `posts.search` and `posts.store`
+  carry `agent` metadata in [routes/web.ts](../../routes/web.ts); their
+  input schemas, validation and the `PostPolicy` check are the ones the
+  website uses. A tool call re-enters the app as an HTTP request.
+- **Tool names are `posts_search` and `posts_store`** (`agent.toolName`),
+  because some assistants drop names containing a dot. Route names are
+  unchanged.
+- **Only search is read-only.** `posts_store` keeps the default hints for
+  a writing tool, so an assistant asks its user before calling it.
+- **`@guren/plugin-mcp` serves them at `/mcp`**, registered in
+  [src/app.ts](../../src/app.ts). Callers authenticate with a bearer
+  token; a call without one answers 401.
+- **Tokens live in the `api_tokens` table**, through the framework's
+  `DatabaseApiTokenStore`, configured in
+  [AuthProvider](../../app/Providers/AuthProvider.ts) with the `users`
+  provider so a token resolves to the full user record. Ops issue them
+  with `bunx guren token:issue --name <n> --user <id> --tools <scopes>`.
+- **`posts.store` answers a JSON caller with `201` and the created post**
+  and keeps the redirect for the form. A tool call has no page to follow
+  a redirect to, and the assistant needs the new post's id.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A change to the search or post rules reaches assistants without a
+  second edit; so does a mistake.
+- Exposing another route is a deliberate edit to its `agent` metadata.
+  `bunx guren tool:list` shows what is exposed.
+- An API client that posts to `/posts` with `Accept: application/json`
+  now gets `201` and a body where it got a redirect.
+- Rate limits are the plugin's defaults and are kept in process memory,
+  so they are per instance.
+- There is no page for users to manage their own tokens, no approval
+  queue and no audit sink yet.
